course: biology
lesson: 49
title: Babies: eggs, live birth, pouches and parents
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Animals have babies in three main ways: they lay eggs that hatch outside, they give birth to live young that grew inside the mother, or, like kangaroos, they give birth to tiny babies that finish growing in a pouch.
explain: Some parents never meet their babies, like the sea turtle that lays her eggs in the sand and swims away, while others work for months, like the emperor penguin dad who keeps an egg warm on his feet all winter.
explain: If your school has an incubator, watch a chick peck its way out with the tiny hard tip on its beak after about 21 days: it is the same egg you could have had for breakfast, but with a rooster in the farmyard.
explain: Cartoons give every baby a mum who does all the work and a stork that brings it; in real life the seahorse dad carries the eggs, the cuckoo mum tricks other birds into raising her chick, and the crocodile mum carries her babies gently in her jaws.
fact: A red kangaroo is born after only about 33 days, the size of a jellybean, crawls into its mother's pouch to drink milk, and stays there about 8 months.
fact: An emperor penguin father keeps the egg on his feet under a fold of belly skin for about 2 months of Antarctic winter, eating nothing, while the mother feeds at sea.
fact: In seahorses the mother puts her eggs into a pouch on the father's belly, and after a few weeks he pushes out the babies, sometimes hundreds or even over a thousand.
fact: A chicken egg takes about 21 days to hatch if kept warm; the chick cracks the shell with a hard tip on its beak called the egg tooth, and supermarket eggs never hatch because no rooster was there.
fact: Cuckoos lay their eggs in other birds' nests; the cuckoo chick pushes the other eggs out, and its loud begging, which sounds like a whole nest of hungry chicks, makes the foster parents, such as reed warblers, feed a chick much bigger than themselves.
fact: An elephant carries her baby for about 22 months, longer than any other land animal, because a big animal with a big brain needs a long time to grow; the calf weighs about 100 kg and is looked after by its mother, aunts, sisters and grandmothers.
fact: A newborn blue whale is about 7 metres long and drinks very rich milk, often quoted at about 380 litres a day; whale babies are born live under water, usually tail first.
fact: A sea turtle lays about 100 eggs in the sand at night and never sees her babies; after about two months the hatchlings dig out and head for the brightest horizon, which on a natural beach is the sea.
fact: In sea turtles and crocodiles the warmth of the nest decides whether babies become male or female, and bright lights on beaches can lead turtle hatchlings the wrong way.
fact: A mother crocodile digs open her nest when the babies squeak inside the eggs, carries them gently to the water in her mouth and guards them for weeks or months.
fact: A giraffe is born with a drop of about 1.5 to 2 metres to the ground, is already about 1.8 metres tall, and usually stands within about an hour; the drop helps break the birth sac and start the baby breathing.
fact: The adder, Britain's only venomous snake, gives birth to live young, each in a thin skin it breaks out of at once; a mother keeping eggs inside can warm them by basking, and some sharks also give birth to live young.
fact: A newborn human baby has about 270 to 300 bones and pieces of cartilage, many of which fuse to leave 206 in an adult, and human babies need their parents for many years.
exercise: What are the three main ways animals have babies? | Laying eggs, live birth, and tiny babies finishing in a pouch | Eggs, seeds and spores, the same three ways that plants use | Live birth only, in all animals | Eggs for small animals and pouches for big ones
exercise: How big is a newborn red kangaroo? | About the size of a jellybean | About the size of a big pet cat | About the size of a small football | As tall as its mother's knee already
exercise: How long does a joey stay in its mother's pouch? | About 8 months | Only about 8 days | About 8 whole years | It never goes into the pouch
exercise: Who keeps the emperor penguin egg warm through the winter? | The father, on his feet under a fold of skin | The mother, in a nest of stones on the ice | Both parents, in a burrow under the snow | Nobody, it is buried in the ice
exercise: Which parent carries the eggs in seahorses? | The father, in a pouch on his belly | The mother, in her mouth | Both, taking turns | Neither, they float free in the sea
exercise: How does a chick get out of its egg? | It pecks through the shell with a hard tip on its beak, the egg tooth | Its mother hears it tapping and cracks the shell open for it with her beak | The shell melts after 21 days | It kicks its way out with its feet
exercise: Why will supermarket eggs never hatch? | They come from hens kept without roosters | They are kept too warm on the shelves | Their shells are too thick for any chick to break | They are made in a factory
exercise: Why does a reed warbler feed a cuckoo chick much bigger than itself? | Its loud begging sounds like a whole nest of hungry chicks | The warbler knows it is a cuckoo and feels sorry for it | The cuckoo mother pays it with worms | The warbler is really the cuckoo chick's own mother, just smaller
exercise: How long does an elephant carry her baby before it is born? | About 22 months | About 9 months, like people | Only about 2 months | About 5 whole years
exercise: Who helps look after a baby elephant? | Its mother, aunts, sisters and grandmothers | Nobody, it is left alone at once to look after itself | Only its father | Other animals such as zebras
exercise: How is a blue whale calf born? | Live under water, usually tail first | From an egg laid on a warm, sandy beach | From an egg floating out in the open sea | In a warm pouch on its mother's belly
exercise: What does a sea turtle mother do after laying her eggs? | Covers them and returns to the sea, never seeing her babies | Sits on them for two months | Carries them gently in her mouth down to the sea when they hatch | Stays on the beach to guard them
exercise: How do sea turtle hatchlings find the sea? | They head for the brightest horizon, which is the sea on a wild beach | They follow their mother's footprints in the sand, left from the night she laid them | They smell the seaweed | They listen to the waves only
exercise: How does a crocodile mother carry her babies to the water? | Gently in her mouth | On her back like a boat | Under her front legs | She does not, they walk alone
exercise: How soon can a newborn giraffe usually stand? | Within about an hour | After about a whole week | After about a whole month | It cannot stand for a year
exercise: How does a baby adder come into the world? | It is born live, in a thin skin it breaks out of at once | It hatches from an egg buried in sand | It grows in a pouch | It splits off from its mother's tail
exercise: Why does a newborn baby have more bones than an adult? | Many small bones fuse together as the child grows | Adults lose bones when they get old | Babies have extra bones that fall out with milk teeth | They do not, babies have fewer bones
test: «storks bring babies, that's why they're on the cards» What is right? | Babies grow inside their mothers; the stork is just an old story | Right, storks bring all babies | Right, but only in Europe, where the storks nest on the roofs | Storks bring only baby birds
test: In a cartoon the penguin mum sits on the egg all winter while dad goes fishing. What really happens with emperor penguins? | The dad keeps the egg warm while the mum feeds at sea | Exactly what the cartoon shows | Both parents leave the egg in the snow | The egg is kept warm in the sea
test: «that seahorse is well fat, she's having babies» What is right? | Probably he: seahorse fathers carry the eggs in a pouch | Right, only female seahorses swell up | It is just a fat seahorse that ate too much | Seahorses have no babies at all, they just split into two halves
test: «a croc carrying babies in its mouth, it's eating them!!» What is really happening? | The mother is gently carrying her babies to the water | She is eating them one by one | She is cooling them in her mouth | She is stealing another crocodile's babies to raise as her own
test: What would happen to turtle hatchlings on a beach next to a brightly lit hotel? | They might crawl towards the lights instead of the sea | Nothing, they always find the sea by smell | They would hatch much faster in the warm light | They would all become male
test: A crocodile nest is in a sunny, very warm spot this year. What could change for the babies? | More may become one sex than the other, as warmth decides it | They will hatch with longer teeth | They will all be twins | Nothing, warmth does not matter at all once the eggs have been laid
test: «why's the baby giraffe dropped on the ground, that's rough» What is the best answer? | The drop helps break the birth sac and start it breathing | The mother dropped it by mistake | Giraffes are born from eggs in trees | It is a test of whether the baby is strong enough to live in the wild
test: «my uncle says he can hatch a chick from a supermarket egg with a heat lamp» What is right? | It won't work: supermarket eggs come from hens with no rooster | It works after exactly 21 days | It works if the egg is brown | It works if you turn it every hour and keep it damp with warm water
test: In a game, the kangaroo baby is born big and hops out to eat grass at once. What is wrong? | It is born the size of a jellybean and grows in the pouch for months | Nothing, that is right | Kangaroos lay eggs in the pouch and the eggs hatch into baby kangaroos | Kangaroo babies are born in trees
test: «snakes all lay eggs, easy» What is right? | Not all: the adder gives birth to live young | Right, every snake lays eggs, no exceptions | Right, and so do all sharks | No, all snakes are born live
test: An elephant and a mouse both give birth to live babies. Why does the elephant take so long? | A big animal with a big brain needs a long time to grow | Elephants forget they are pregnant | Elephant babies sleep for most of it and so grow very slowly | It is the same time as a mouse
test: «human babies are just useless lol» What is the honest science? | Human babies are among the most helpless and need parents for years | Human babies can walk within an hour | Human babies need no care at all | Human babies grow up faster than any other animal on the whole planet
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reproduction of the red kangaroo? | The young is born minute, after a brief period, and completes its growth in the pouch | The young is hatched from an egg deposited within the pouch of the mother some weeks before | The young is born at a size approaching that of the mother | The young is reared by the father upon his feet
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the chick of the cuckoo is fed by birds of another species. | Its loud begging resembles the calls of a whole brood, so the foster parents work harder | The foster parents recognise it as kin and choose to feed it | The cuckoo provides food to the foster parents in exchange | The foster parents are compelled to do so by the adult cuckoo, which watches over the nest
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the seahorse. | The female places her eggs in a pouch upon the male, who carries them until they hatch | The female carries the eggs within her mouth | The eggs are abandoned upon the sea floor | Both parents carry the eggs alternately upon their backs until the young are able to swim
exam: Ascertain the period for which an elephant carries her young prior to birth. | Approximately twenty-two months | Approximately nine months | Approximately two months | Approximately four and a half years
